00:07:58.994 --> 00:08:05.174
doesn't look Thai or Laotian or, uh,
you know, Vietnamese, even Chinese.

00:08:05.784 --> 00:08:09.945
Like what do you think is more
difficult for a, uh, say a European

00:08:09.945 --> 00:08:15.945
language native speaker to learn those
languages that seem exotic to us, or

00:08:15.945 --> 00:08:20.085
is it easier for those people to learn
what is probably to them a more exotic

00:08:20.085 --> 00:08:22.635
language, English or French or Spanish?

00:08:23.174 --> 00:08:23.984
Um...
Which way is it?

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.460
I think it's expected in the
west that anyone going there

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.360
will learn the language.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:33.600
If they're living in the country
over here, you get what's called

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.430
these ex-pat bubbles, I guess.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:38.039
And which, you know, what's the difference
between an ex-pat and an immigrant?

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:44.820
Um, but these ex-pat bubbles where
people live 20, 30 years without having

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.730
to speak a word of the local language.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:52.665
So this has becomes a normal, um,
what I have found, however, and since

00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:57.735
day one, because I knew a lot of
languages that feed into Thai as well

00:08:57.735 --> 00:09:00.045
as like old Chinese, middle Chinese.

00:09:00.045 --> 00:09:04.905
And then, um, from my dad's side, so
Hindi and Sanskrit and other Indic

00:09:04.905 --> 00:09:09.630
languages, Thai was actually quite
easy, um, to, to get up to speed.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:13.320
I could write it in an afternoon
from my understanding of

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.900
Devanagari, um, and Sanskrit.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:19.200
And so within months I
was up speaking Thai.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:24.930
And the thing that I find is, it's not
difficult, but you just have to get beyond

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:29.850
that, but it's understanding the system
and it is that system that's based on...

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.770
and even Chinese is based on, I dunno if
you've ever looked at their ... system

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:37.140
in Chinese, but this is also based
on the map of the human mouth,

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.510
which is the Devanagari or Sanskrit.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.665
Uh Brahmic, um, And so
everything from tones...

00:09:44.665 --> 00:09:51.564
tones in Mandarin, a parallel one-to-one
in these boxes that you have Thai

00:09:51.585 --> 00:09:55.785
tones and Vietnamese and anything,
um, is actually based on this map,

00:09:55.814 --> 00:09:57.265
which is traced back to Sanskrit.

00:09:57.585 --> 00:10:00.765
Believe it or not, even the
Korean writing is based on

00:10:00.765 --> 00:10:02.505
this same format from Sanskrit.

00:10:02.835 --> 00:10:09.225
So having that, it's not a hard jump,
but if you're going into Thai say as your

00:10:09.225 --> 00:10:15.180
first Asian language and not having that
backup, it is like, what the heck is this?

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:19.860
Because most native speakers of Thai don't
actually understand the system because the

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.540
native speakers, nobody ever taught them.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:22.100
Right.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:23.130
And even in the schools.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:27.180
And so the way they learn is by
rote and just by memorizing rules,

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.830
that seemingly don't make sense.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:33.810
So that is a huge challenge, but actually
Thai is not a difficult language to,

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:37.380
to learn and to develop prosody in.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.590
And this is the key I think,
is developing this process.

00:10:41.594 --> 00:10:45.854
And prosody always trumps what
you look like on the outside.

00:10:46.305 --> 00:10:50.175
If I go into a Seven Eleven and
I can speak in a way that feels

00:10:50.175 --> 00:10:54.594
natural, um, they will instantly
just speak to me normally.

00:10:55.425 --> 00:10:58.035
And there are some tricks that
you can throw some cues in.

00:10:58.425 --> 00:11:04.734
Um, that will, uh, for example,
if you go in, um ... you don't

00:11:04.755 --> 00:11:06.405
need to put that in a plastic bag.

00:11:06.865 --> 00:11:11.114
And the way that I say that is a
colloquial way that anyone else

00:11:11.114 --> 00:11:14.415
would say it and they might even be
looking at your face and then the

00:11:14.415 --> 00:11:18.214
whole interaction will just be in Thai
and go without any surprise at all.

00:11:19.395 --> 00:11:21.915
There, there is a tendency for
them to speak to you in English,

00:11:21.974 --> 00:11:23.055
as soon as they look at you?

00:11:23.055 --> 00:11:32.463
Yes, so this gets back
to then your question.

00:11:32.463 --> 00:11:33.638
The big challenge is, and most poeple
say oh they won't speak Thai to me.

00:11:33.638 --> 00:11:34.186
Um, or they just won't
speak Chinese to me.

00:11:34.186 --> 00:11:34.447
Maybe you've had that where people
will just speak English to you.

00:11:34.447 --> 00:11:35.762
And so that is one of
the biggest challenges.

00:11:36.152 --> 00:11:36.352
Yeah.

00:11:36.352 --> 00:11:39.080
I found that to be a function of
how well you speak the language.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:40.580
I never get that in Chinese.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:41.870
I never get it in Japanese.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:43.710
I shouldn't say never, but very rarely.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:44.700
Yeah.
I agree.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.410
Um, you know, if the person assumes
that you speak their language better

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:52.310
than they speak your language, they'll
happily, most people are happy to use

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:53.780
the language that makes the most sense.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.840
Uh, let me ask you a question,
what advice now, so a lot of those

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:02.235
languages are, uh, so someone who
wants to learn a tonal language,

00:12:02.265 --> 00:12:07.574
whether it be Mandarin or Cantonese
or, uh, you know, Thai or whatever,

00:12:08.265 --> 00:12:10.655
Burmese is tonal as well, isn't it?

00:12:10.655 --> 00:12:11.155
Yeah

00:12:11.484 --> 00:12:11.984
Yeah.

00:12:12.114 --> 00:12:15.194
So, and, and presumably they're
not going to learn the Sanskrit,

00:12:15.285 --> 00:12:17.115
uh, rules or whatever it was.

00:12:17.475 --> 00:12:21.495
Uh, what sort of general bits of
advice do you have for people?

00:12:22.335 --> 00:12:25.305
Presumably you just said, they're not
going to learn the sanscript rules.

00:12:25.605 --> 00:12:28.844
Um, I that's why I've set Mindkraft up.

00:12:29.055 --> 00:12:32.444
Actually, you're insane not
to learn the Sanskrit rules.

00:12:33.015 --> 00:12:33.584
Okay.

00:12:33.675 --> 00:12:38.625
Um, because, and this is the other thing
tones in English, we think of tones.

00:12:38.775 --> 00:12:41.415
The word tone has a
connotation with pitch.

00:12:42.194 --> 00:12:44.324
Tones are not pitch.

00:12:45.525 --> 00:12:49.185
And I will say that again, tones
are not pitch, tones are throat.

00:12:50.625 --> 00:12:52.814
And the pitch is a
by-product of the throat.

00:12:52.995 --> 00:12:55.694
So you, and you get these people
learning tonal languages and they'll

00:12:55.694 --> 00:12:58.995
leave and get these graphs and try
and plot the frequencies of their

00:12:59.405 --> 00:13:02.055
actual pitch to match native speakers.

00:13:02.295 --> 00:13:03.645
And they still get the tone wrong.

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:06.944
They still get the confused looks on
the face and it's especially apparent

00:13:06.944 --> 00:13:12.314
in Vietnamese because tones are a whole
bunch of different things that are going

00:13:12.314 --> 00:13:16.214
on in the throat, which are actually
traced back to that Sanskrit map.

00:13:16.454 --> 00:13:17.535
It's not a difficult map.

00:13:17.574 --> 00:13:18.074
You've got...

00:13:18.965 --> 00:13:20.729
Was Sanskrit a tonal language?

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:22.939
Um, no, it wasn't.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.280
Well, you actually had pitches, so
similar to pitch, actually accent.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:30.209
However, the it's, this is
the whole thing, because tones

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:32.219
are not pitches in English.

00:13:32.219 --> 00:13:36.390
We use this stupid word tone,
which forces everybody to focus

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:38.280
on something to do with pitch.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.430
And it's not, you focus on the throat.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.800
And so if I say like
I've got normal voice...

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:47.069
you see the voices, just...

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:48.990
and it glides off.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:50.740
And then I've got stiff voice...

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.790
my voice cuts it.

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:55.470
And then I've got creaky voice...

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.890
where it ratchets down at the end.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:03.495
They are the only three tones
originally in any tonal language,

00:14:03.495 --> 00:14:06.435
whether it's Burmese, whether it's
Chinese, whether the Vietnamese,

00:14:06.435 --> 00:14:08.535
whether it's Thai, Thai languages.

00:14:08.955 --> 00:14:10.905
And then you've got registers.

00:14:10.905 --> 00:14:12.375
So you've got breathy voice.

